WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.834
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy-nominated comedian

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:04.501
and actor you know from his work
in "Big Mouth"

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:06.067
and "Don't Worry Darling" and
"Sing."

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:07.868
He executive produces and stars

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:09.334
in "History of the World:
Part II,"

00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:11.100
which is streaming now on Hulu.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.367
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:14.133
-Luke, there it is.

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:16.767
-Judas, what's going down?
-How you doing, man?

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:18.400
-Man, I had to get me something
in my belly, man.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.133
You know, that last supper
wasn't [bleep].

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:22.100
-Yeah, didn't hit anything
for me.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.033
Yeah.
More like a last snack, right?

00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:26.567
-Nothing worse than going
somewhere for a meal

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:28.767
and you got to come to [bleep]
home and eat again.

00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:30.734
-It's like, what's the point?
I could have just stayed home.

00:00:30.734 --> 00:00:33.167
-What's the [bleep] point, man?
-Jesus invites us over for

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:35.534
dinner, and then what, it's like
a bunch of small mini plates.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:37.334
One lamb meatball as an entree?

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:38.634
-If you're going to give me
one meatball,

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.100
it better be a big-ass meatball.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.434
-If it's not bigger than
my head, I don't want it.

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:45.100
-Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.667
Nick Kroll, everybody.

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:48.100
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:57.167
♪♪

00:00:57.167 --> 00:01:06.334
♪♪

00:01:06.334 --> 00:01:08.033
So lovely to have you back,
Nick.

00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:10.267
-Thank you for having me, Seth.
-It's great to have you.

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.767
How's your family?
Are they good?

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:14.100
-Yeah, I'd rather not talk about
my family, my kid.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.868
Just trying to stay private with
that stuff.

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.434
-You can just say they're fine.
You know?

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:20.934
-Oh, they're fine.
-Okay. Great.

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:23.167
[ Laughter ]
That's so much less aggressive.

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.434
-My son did the
funniest thing recently.

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:26.901
-Oh, so now you do want to talk
about it?

00:01:26.901 --> 00:01:28.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.834
-On my terms.
-Your terms.

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:32.834
This is so exciting.

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:35.467
First of all, I remember seeing
this movie when I was a kid,

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.300
"History of the World: Part I"
which was very funny,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.601
because there was an expectation
that there would be a part two.

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:41.567
And it's taken now
over 30 years.

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:44.367
-42 years to be exact, yes.

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:46.501
-And so this is a Mel Brooks
film.

00:01:46.501 --> 00:01:48.734
-And my son loves Mel Brooks.
-Oh, my God,

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:51.734
so now we are going to talk --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:55.234
I feel like if I had asked,
you would've had a different --

00:01:55.234 --> 00:01:56.634
Does your son like Mel Brooks?

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:57.734
-I don't want to talk about
that.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:00.234
-Okay, gotcha.
-I have my public life.

00:02:00.234 --> 00:02:01.868
I am who I am here
on television,

00:02:01.868 --> 00:02:03.133
and then I have my private life,
my family at home, okay?

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:05.801
-Okay, it feels like that's
really a gray area.

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:08.033
-So, when Mel comes over to
babysit --

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:09.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:11.367
This is a story about Mel.
It's not about my kid.

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:12.534
-Okay, gotcha.
-I keep private.

00:02:12.534 --> 00:02:14.901
It's very separate, okay?

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.234
No, I've been a Mel Brooks fan
my entire life.

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:18.734
I assume you as well.

00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:22.534
And I met him for the first time
like 20 years ago.

00:02:22.534 --> 00:02:24.234
And I was at an event.

00:02:24.234 --> 00:02:25.367
And I went up to him,
and I said,

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:26.934
"Mr. Brooks, I just want to say
hello."

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:28.634
He's like, "Hello."
And I said, "I have an idea

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:30.334
for a remake of
'The Producers.'"

00:02:30.334 --> 00:02:32.334
And he said,
"Go do your own work."

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:33.767
And then he just walked away.

00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:36.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.701
So, 20 years later, look who
comes [bleep] crawling back.

00:02:40.701 --> 00:02:44.934
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:50.167
No, but the truth is, I mean,
I was I took that to heart.

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:53.067
"Go do your own work."
When I first moved to New York,

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:55.400
I was doing, like, background
work, extra stuff.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.934
I would try to be
in commercials.

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:59.734
And I was told, if you get your
face on camera in a commercial,

00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:01.634
you get a full fee, so, I'd be,
like,

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:04.300
in an office commercial
for Xerox.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.901
You can just feel me in each
take just sort of being like

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:11.400
Sliding into frame.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.767
I don't know where
Seth's camera is.

00:03:12.767 --> 00:03:14.300
-Yeah, here, take
a single of me.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.601
Where's my single of me?

00:03:15.601 --> 00:03:18.367
Alright, ready? Here.
-If it's a Xerox commercial,

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:19.834
and I'd just sort of be like...

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:23.467
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:26.667
...getting fired from commercial
after commercial.

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:30.534
But I did do extra work,
background work.

00:03:30.534 --> 00:03:34.334
I did do background work
when the season of

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:37.467
"Curb Your Enthusiasm" when
Larry was playing Max Bialystock

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:42.567
in "The Producers" on Broadway,
I was in the crowd in

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:45.601
the St. James Theatre doing
extra work

00:03:45.601 --> 00:03:50.667
for a Mel Brooks musical, as
portrayed by Larry David in

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.334
"Curb Your Enthusiasm."

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:58.767
So, now I'm doing "Curb Your
Judaism," again,

00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:00.834
as a [bleep] you
to both of those guys.

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:03.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:05.434
-The amount of Jewish layers
that you just made.

00:04:05.434 --> 00:04:07.167
-Oh, my God, yes.

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:12.601
It's just like a moist, like,
matzah meal thing.

00:04:12.601 --> 00:04:15.434
-So you were playing sort of --
that is "Curb Your Judaism."

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:16.868
That is the sketch
we saw a clip from.

00:04:16.868 --> 00:04:18.767
You're playing a Larry David
type.

00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:22.634
The actual J.B. Smoove is
playing J.B. Smoove's part.

00:04:22.634 --> 00:04:25.200
Did you ever think that you
should reach out to Larry

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.501
and see if he wanted to play
Larry?

00:04:26.501 --> 00:04:29.234
-I thought the most respectful
thing I could do was

00:04:29.234 --> 00:04:33.000
not make Larry David say no
to me, you know.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.501
Like, there's just no world --
-He was never going to say yes.

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:39.234
-The nicest thing I could do is
not even bother him with it.

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:41.267
But I was like,
I don't know if you can do

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:43.901
"Curb Your Enthusiasm"
without J.B. Smoove.

00:04:43.901 --> 00:04:45.734
-Right.
-Because I think like, I mean,

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:48.467
J.B. is genuinely like the
funniest person on the planet.

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:50.467
-And singular.
-Yes, yes.

00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:54.534
And so it just felt like a great
way for us to kind of, like,

00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:58.067
to bring it all together.
-And it should be noted,

00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:59.934
Richard Kind is in the sketch,
who's been on "Curb."

00:04:59.934 --> 00:05:03.033
You have, since doing background
work, a real triumph.

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.367
You were then also then on
"Curb."

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:07.467
-I've been on "Curb,"
and I have also --

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:10.767
and Larry David was on
"The League" as future me.

00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:14.367
-So there you go.
-So, in a way,

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:16.267
he's already been on the show.

00:05:16.267 --> 00:05:18.634
-Yeah. He really has.

00:05:18.634 --> 00:05:22.133
You guys were at
opening night -- sorry,

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:23.801
the premiere, I should say,
not opening night.

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:27.734
This is a photo of you and Ike
and Dave Stassen

00:05:27.734 --> 00:05:30.634
and Mel and Wanda. And how is
Mel on the red carpet?

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:32.434
I mean, he's obviously done
quite a few over his life.

00:05:32.434 --> 00:05:34.868
-I mean, Mel is amazing.
He's 96.

00:05:34.868 --> 00:05:36.367
He's incredibly sharp,
incredibly funny.

00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:37.667
But he's also very much
Mel Brooks.

00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:41.167
So he won't go -- The
photographer is like, "Okay,

00:05:41.167 --> 00:05:42.968
look at me, look at me, look at
me, look at me, look at me."

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:44.667
And Mel goes, "We're all looking
at you.

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:45.934
Take the [bleep] picture."

00:05:45.934 --> 00:05:49.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:50.968
And I was like, it's so funny.

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:52.501
And he's saying what we all --

00:05:52.501 --> 00:05:55.601
It's like we're all just waiting
to be 96 to be as honest as Mel.

00:05:55.601 --> 00:05:56.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.901 --> 00:05:59.501
-But I would imagine that
honesty -- sometimes you're

00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:01.834
there and you're enjoying him
being honest with other people.

00:06:01.834 --> 00:06:04.534
But also through this process,
you would pitch him jokes.

00:06:04.534 --> 00:06:07.467
-Yeah.
-How was Mel receiving joke
pitches?

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:09.968
-He was actually great.
Like, he narrates the show,

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:13.968
and so I was directing him in --
you know, in the original film,

00:06:13.968 --> 00:06:16.000
Orson Welles does
all the narration.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.868
We had Mel narrate this one.

00:06:17.868 --> 00:06:20.667
And so, he'll announce like,
"The Civil War!"

00:06:20.667 --> 00:06:23.100
And we'll have him do --
I was like, "Do one surprised."

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.767
And he's like, "oh, funny."
He'll go, "The Civil War?!"

00:06:25.767 --> 00:06:27.501
And then I'll be like,
"Now do one angry."

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:28.934
And he'll go,
"No, that's stupid."

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:30.934
[ Laughter ]
"It's a bad pitch."

00:06:30.934 --> 00:06:33.868
And I was, "Oh, cool."
So it's like, literally my

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:37.234
all-time hero being -- it was
just a roller coaster of

00:06:37.234 --> 00:06:40.567
emotions, which is like what
it's like having a son.

00:06:40.567 --> 00:06:42.300
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.501
I'm going to give you space.
I'm going to give you space

00:06:44.501 --> 00:06:46.434
to tell a story if you want to
tell a story.

00:06:46.434 --> 00:06:49.033
-Talk about my son?
-Okay.

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:50.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.000
-The guy's got no boundaries.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.234
It's like he's like TMZ
at the airport.

00:06:55.234 --> 00:06:56.634
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, my God, come on.

00:06:56.634 --> 00:06:58.601
I've known you a long time.
How dare you.

00:06:58.601 --> 00:07:00.567
[ Laughter ]

00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:03.467
-You don't know the black track
suits that he normally wears.

00:07:03.467 --> 00:07:06.334
-Yeah, I like to hang out -- I
just like to hang out

00:07:06.334 --> 00:07:07.667
at the luggage claim by LAX.

00:07:07.667 --> 00:07:10.033
That's just a thing I do anyway.
-Very true.

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:15.567
-Johnny Knoxville plays Rasputin
in a sketch that sort of weaves

00:07:15.567 --> 00:07:17.400
its way through called
Jack Rasp.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.501
-Yes.
-He reached out to you.

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:21.133
-He reached out.
When we announced that we were

00:07:21.133 --> 00:07:23.334
doing the show, he was like,
"Mel is my hero.

00:07:23.334 --> 00:07:26.400
If there's anything I can do on
the show, I'll happily do it."

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.834
And we were doing this whole
Russian revolution thing.

00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:30.734
Danny DeVito plays our Nicholas.

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:33.501
Dove Cameron plays
Princess Anastasia.

00:07:33.501 --> 00:07:35.901
Jack Black plays Joseph Stalin.

00:07:35.901 --> 00:07:38.033
[ Laughter ]

00:07:38.033 --> 00:07:39.400
Rob Corddry plays Lenin.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.968
The fastest casting I've ever
done in my life.

00:07:41.968 --> 00:07:44.267
I've been waiting 20 years to be
like, "Corddry's Lenin.

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:45.968
That's it, we're done,
moving on."

00:07:45.968 --> 00:07:49.067
So, and we get to
Johnny Knoxville as Rasputin

00:07:49.067 --> 00:07:51.801
and doing Jack Rasp.

00:07:51.801 --> 00:07:54.267
So he said, "If you need anyone
for anything else" --

00:07:54.267 --> 00:07:58.067
Rasputin was famously --
They tried to kill Rasputin

00:07:58.067 --> 00:07:59.968
famously over and over.

00:07:59.968 --> 00:08:03.133
So who better to do that than
Knoxville who's been doing it?

00:08:03.133 --> 00:08:04.400
And when I called him,
I was like,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.601
"Hey, do you want to play
Rasputin?"

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:07.367
He was like, "Oh, great, I would
love to.

00:08:07.367 --> 00:08:13.300
I was at a sex shop/urologist in
Saint Petersburg, Russia,

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:16.834
and I saw Rasputin's penis
in a jar."

00:08:16.834 --> 00:08:18.200
[ Laughter ]

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.033
And then he sent me
a picture of it.

00:08:20.033 --> 00:08:22.434
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.434 --> 00:08:25.033
-At that point, it's fate.
-I know. It was perfect.

00:08:25.033 --> 00:08:26.767
And my son loved the idea of --
-Okay.

00:08:26.767 --> 00:08:28.634
[ Laughter ]

00:08:28.634 --> 00:08:30.501
-We do all of our castings
together.

00:08:30.501 --> 00:08:32.434
He watches tape with me.

00:08:32.434 --> 00:08:35.000
And, um...
-Mm-hmm.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.334
[ Laughter ]

00:08:37.334 --> 00:08:38.534
-I don't know what
you're waiting for.

00:08:38.534 --> 00:08:41.067
-Nothing.
I'm just going to move on.

00:08:41.067 --> 00:08:43.434
Thank you for sharing that?

00:08:43.434 --> 00:08:45.167
-Okay.
Now, this feels condescending.

00:08:45.167 --> 00:08:47.200
[ Laughter ]

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:52.033
Can I tell a story about
my character?

00:08:52.033 --> 00:08:53.667
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:08:53.667 --> 00:08:55.968
[ Laughter ]
-From the show?

00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:57.267
-Yeah.
Gerena says hi, by the way.

00:08:57.267 --> 00:08:58.434
-Oh, great. Give her my love.
-I will.

00:08:58.434 --> 00:09:00.868
-Okay.
-So I --

00:09:00.868 --> 00:09:03.300
My character's name
in the Russian revolution is

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.234
named Schmuck Mudman.
-Schmuck Mudman.

00:09:05.234 --> 00:09:06.968
-Yeah.
-Which, I will say, has

00:09:06.968 --> 00:09:09.701
a real nice
Nick Kroll sheen to it.

00:09:09.701 --> 00:09:11.968
Only Nick Kroll would come up
with the name Schmuck Mudman.

00:09:11.968 --> 00:09:14.434
-Well, it's like I build my
characters from the name up.

00:09:14.434 --> 00:09:17.100
Other people are like, "What
were their childhood like?"

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.133
No, I'm like, "Just his name is
Schmuck Mudman."

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:21.267
That's what matters.

00:09:21.267 --> 00:09:24.100
That was not the first -- There
were a couple other ideas

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:27.000
that we had for a name.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.501
Putz [Bleep]stein
was the other name.

00:09:30.501 --> 00:09:32.434
And then we did some research
and we found out that

00:09:32.434 --> 00:09:36.100
that was Clark Gable's name
in "Gone With the Wind."

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:37.267
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:37.267 --> 00:09:38.667
-And you know that famous line.

00:09:38.667 --> 00:09:40.467
It's, "Frankly, my dear,
I don't give a name."

00:09:40.467 --> 00:09:41.734
Let me do that again.

00:09:41.734 --> 00:09:43.868
[ Laughter ]

00:09:43.868 --> 00:09:46.901
Famously, Clark Gable's name
in "Gone With the Wind."

00:09:46.901 --> 00:09:48.667
-Yeah.
-And that famous line we all

00:09:48.667 --> 00:09:51.000
know is, "Frankly, my dear,
I don't give a damn,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.000
or my name isn't
[Bleep] Putstein."

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.067
[ Laughter ]
I didn't even say it right.

00:09:55.067 --> 00:09:56.701
I [bleep] the whole thing up.
-Yeah.

00:09:56.701 --> 00:09:58.801
[ Laughter ]

00:09:58.801 --> 00:10:01.133
-Can I do it one more time?
-No, I don't think so.

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:04.267
Trust me. They have been
watching this all night.

00:10:04.267 --> 00:10:06.434
They do not stand for flubs.
-Okay.

00:10:06.434 --> 00:10:09.300
[ Laughter ]

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.767
I was doing cocaine with
this bear backstage, so...

00:10:11.767 --> 00:10:13.901
[ Laughter ]
-That's what they like.

00:10:13.901 --> 00:10:15.300
